    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "The fact that the panel was entirely male, combined with the disparity in experience and the vague justification given, is a valid reason to question the objectivity of the process.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what that panel composition might signify, how organizations evaluate these roles, and how you might strategically handle the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is an all-male panel a sign of anything?\n\n* **It is not definitive proof of discrimination on its own:** In senior engineering consultancy roles, demographic imbalances mean interview panels are frequently all-male simply reflecting the existing leadership makeup. From a legal or procedural standpoint, an all-male panel alone does not prove sex discrimination.\n* **It is a recognized risk factor for bias:** Best-practice HR guidelines specifically discourage homogeneous panels because they significantly increase the risk of **affinity bias** (the tendency to favor candidates who resemble or relate easily to the interviewers) and informal \"cultural fit\" assessments over objective scoring.\n* **Subjective criteria can mask bias:** When a candidate with 15 years of experience, chartered status, and major project leadership is passed over for someone with roughly half that experience on the basis of *\"excellent client relationships\"*, it raises questions. While commercial skills and rainmaking are crucial for department heads, \"client relationships\" is often subjective and can become a proxy for personal rapport or shared networks.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why haven't they responded in two weeks?\n\nA two-week silence after an interview at this level is poor practice, but common for a few reasons:\n* **Administrative inertia:** Finalizing appointments, reference checks, and contract sign-offs often delays formal feedback.\n* **Reluctance / Liability fears:** Panels often struggle to write feedback for close runner-ups, particularly when they know the unsuccessful candidate was objectively more experienced. HR departments are often wary of committing specific comparative reasons to writing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should you \"make something of it\"?\n\nHow you approach this depends on whether you are an **internal** or **external** candidate, and what outcome you want:\n\n#### A. If your goal is closure and constructive feedback\nDirectly alleging sex bias or challenging the panel’s gender balance at this stage usually leads to immediate defensiveness and generic HR boilerplate. \n\nInstead, send a firm, professional follow-up focusing on **objective competencies**:\n> *\"Dear [HR/Hiring Lead],*\n> \n> *I am following up on my request from two weeks ago regarding written feedback on the Head of Structural Engineering selection process.*\n> \n> *Given the scope of the role, my 15 years of design and project leadership experience, and having reached the final shortlist, understanding the specific competencies or criteria where the successful candidate stood out—particularly regarding commercial versus technical leadership—is important for my professional development.*\n> \n> *I would appreciate a concise written summary of the panel's assessment at your earliest convenience.\"*\n\n#### B. If you are considering a formal grievance or legal claim\nIf you believe unlawful discrimination took place:\n* **Preserve documentation:** Save the job specification, your application, interview notes, the appointment announcement, and all email correspondence.\n* **Check the timeline:** Employment tribunal or grievance deadlines (depending on your jurisdiction, e.g., UK/US/Australia) often have strict time limits (often 3 months less one day from the act of alleged discrimination).\n* **Seek confidential advice first:** Speak to an employment solicitor, your trade union, or an advisory service (such as ACAS in the UK or the EEOC in the US) before raising formal allegations with the firm. They can assess whether the evidence meets the legal threshold for a prima facie case of direct or indirect discrimination.\n\n#### C. Evaluating the company culture\nRegardless of whether bias was conscious, unconscious, or nonexistent:\n* If you are an **internal candidate**, this outcome provides useful data about who gets promoted and what the firm values (or who they feel comfortable with in leadership). If advancement criteria are opaque, your ceiling at this firm may be limited.\n* If you are an **external candidate**, their lack of communication and reliance on subjective criteria may indicate a leadership culture you might have found frustrating to navigate from within.\n",
